<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">El Alamein</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">El Alamein is a town on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast, about 100 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. In 1942 it gave its name to two battles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>: in July the British Eighth Army halted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> advance on Egypt there, and in October and November Montgomery's victory forced Rommel into retreat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/el-alamein.jpg" alt="El Alamein" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Noornenay · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porto_marina_resort_and_spa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>El Alamein is a city in the northern Matrouh Governorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean Sea</a>. It lies 106 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and 300 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. The city is divided into three main areas: El Alamein, Cidi and the village of Tal El Eis. It has a hot desert climate, slightly less hot than the rest of Egypt because of the prevailing winds from the Mediterranean. Its economy rests on petroleum and on tourist areas with hotels and resorts, such as the town of Marina.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Two important battles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> were fought in the area. In the First Battle of El Alamein, from 1 to 27 July 1942, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> blunted the advance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> troops on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and stopped the Italian and German forces that were trying to outflank their position. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-el-alamein">Second Battle of El Alamein</a>, from 23 October to 4 November 1942, Allied forces broke the Axis line and forced the Axis troops back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a>, then British Prime Minister, said of the victory: &quot;Now this is not the end, it is not even the beginning of the end. But it is, perhaps, the end of the beginning.&quot; After the war he wrote: &quot;Before Alamein we never had a victory. After Alamein, we never had a defeat.&quot;</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Military cemeteries lie on Tel el-Eisa Hill outside the town. The German cemetery is an ossuary built in the style of a medieval fortress, and the Italian War Memorial cemetery contains 5,200 graves. There is also a Greek cemetery at El Alamein. The Commonwealth Alamein Memorial and its attached war cemetery, maintained by the Commonwealth War Graves Commission, hold the graves of soldiers from various countries who fought on the Allied side. Each summer many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> travel to El Alamein and the North Coast to escape the heat of other cities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “El Alamein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>British and Commonwealth forces won at <b>El Alamein</b> in 1942, fought in Italy and Burma, and landed in Normandy on D-Day, 6 June 1944.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Montgomery's troops entered Tripoli, three months after <b>El Alamein</b>, as Rommel's forces retreated into Tunisia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-23">23 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1942 British tanks surrounded Abdeen Palace to force Farouk to appoint a Wafd government, and the Axis advance was halted at <b>El Alamein</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-egypt">Kingdom of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
